// Expected: c0e0 at one session, c0e1 at two.
//
// The post-quantum shape of session_psk_cross_feed.vp. Bob's decapsulation
// key is long-term, so every session's ciphertext decapsulates for every
// session's Bob, and nothing in the encapsulation says which run it belongs
// to.
//
// The encapsulation is signed, which is what makes this model specifically
// about sessions. Without that signature there is a one-session attack and
// the cross-session one never gets a chance to matter: an encapsulation key
// is public, so the attacker can encapsulate a shared secret of its own to
// Bob and seal whatever plaintext it likes under it. Signing (ct, e) closes
// that, since forging the signature would need sk, which never leaks. At one
// session the attacker is left with corruption, which fails Bob's SIGNVERIF
// and halts him, so the query holds.
//
// At two sessions it does not have to forge anything. The other session's
// (ct, e, sg) is a genuinely signed triple; relayed as a unit it passes
// SIGNVERIF, decapsulates under the same long-term dk, and leaves Bob holding
// m#2 while Alice holds m. Relaying only part of it desynchronizes the
// signature or the key and merely halts him.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private sk
	apk = PUBKEY(sk)
]

principal Bob[
	knows private dk
	ek = PUBKEY(dk)
]

Alice -> Bob: [apk]
Bob -> Alice: [ek]

principal Alice[
	generates r
	ss, ct = KEM_ENCAP(ek, r)
	generates m
	e = AEAD_ENC(ss, m, nil)
	sg = SIGN(sk, CONCAT(ct, e))
]

Alice -> Bob: ct, e, sg

principal Bob[
	_ = SIGNVERIF(apk, CONCAT(ct, e), sg)?
	ss2 = KEM_DECAP(dk, ct)
	d = AEAD_DEC(ss2, e, nil)?
]

queries[
	confidentiality? m
	equivalence? m, d
]
